UNIT decodeURIComponent
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic

# The module's single default export. A "better decodeURIComponent": behaves like the
# native global decodeURIComponent EXCEPT it (a) maps '+' to space, (b) never throws on
# malformed percent-sequences, instead decoding as much as possible and emitting the
# Unicode REPLACEMENT CHARACTER U+FFFD ('�', shown as the glyph) for certain invalid
# UTF-8 byte sequences, and (c) maps the UTF-16 BOM percent-pairs to two replacement chars.

SIG  decodeURIComponent(encodedURI: string) -> string
     # Throws TypeError when encodedURI is not a string (see BRANCH 0).

DEPENDS-ON  native global decodeURIComponent  # the JS built-in; the unit calls it on the
            # whole string AND on substrings. The native built-in is deterministic and
            # standards-fixed (ECMAScript URI decoding); a blind re-emitter uses the host
            # language's own decodeURIComponent equivalent. No package deps. Zero npm deps.

# =====================================================================================
# EMISSION CONTRACT (for the blind re-emitter)
# =====================================================================================
# Original is CommonJS: `module.exports = function (encodedURI) { ... }` (a DEFAULT export,
# the function itself is the whole module export, not a named property).
# The re-emitter MUST emit an ES module whose DEFAULT export is this function:
#     export default function decodeURIComponent(encodedURI) { ... }
# (or `const fn = ...; export default fn;`). The harness imports the default export and
# calls it as `fn(encodedURI)`. Inputs are single-element arg arrays: [encodedURI].

# =====================================================================================
# CONSTANTS (verbatim — these regexes are load-bearing)
# =====================================================================================
# token        = '%[a-f0-9]{2}'                          # one percent-escape, case-insensitive hex
# singleMatcher = new RegExp('(' + token + ')|([^%]+?)', 'gi')
#                 # global, case-insensitive. Alternation: a single percent-escape, OR a
#                 # MINIMAL (non-greedy) run of one-or-more non-'%' chars. Because the 2nd
#                 # branch is non-greedy '[^%]+?', String.match(singleMatcher) (global) yields
#                 # each non-'%' character as its OWN one-char token. A lone '%' that is not
#                 # part of a valid %XX escape matches NEITHER branch and is DROPPED from the
#                 # match array (e.g. '%'.match(singleMatcher) === null).
# multiMatcher  = new RegExp('(' + token + ')+', 'gi')
#                 # global, case-insensitive. Matches maximal RUNS of consecutive percent-
#                 # escapes (one or more adjacent %XX). NOTE: this regex object is MODULE-LEVEL
#                 # and STATEFUL — it is driven with .exec() in a while-loop (its lastIndex
#                 # advances). It is only ever used inside customDecodeURIComponent, which
#                 # exec-loops it to exhaustion (terminating when exec returns null, which
#                 # also resets lastIndex to 0), so each top-level call starts clean. A
#                 # re-emitter may use a fresh regex per call to get identical behavior.

# =====================================================================================
# BEHAVIOR  (ordered; the public function is BRANCH 0..2, fallback machinery below)
# =====================================================================================

BRANCH 0  TYPE GUARD
  IF typeof encodedURI !== 'string':
     THROW  TypeError  with message exactly:
       "Expected `encodedURI` to be of type `string`, got `" + (typeof encodedURI) + "`"
     # `typeof` words observed: 'number','object' (for null, plain object, array),
     # 'undefined','boolean','function','symbol','bigint'. See SCOPE: non-string args are
     # DOCUMENT-as-OUT (the generator does not emit them; they do not round-trip the codec),
     # but the throw IS the defined behavior and a blind re-emitter MUST reproduce it.

BRANCH 1  PLUS-NORMALIZE + FAST PATH
  encodedURI = encodedURI.replace(/\+/g, ' ')      # EVERY literal '+' -> ' ' (space), GLOBALLY,
                                                    # done BEFORE any decoding. (A '%2B' is NOT a
                                                    # literal '+', so it is untouched here and later
                                                    # decodes to '+'.)
  TRY:
     return (native) decodeURIComponent(encodedURI) # FAST PATH: if the whole (plus-normalized)
                                                    # string is well-formed, return the native decode
                                                    # verbatim. This is the common case (no '%', or
                                                    # all percent-escapes valid).
  CATCH (native threw — string contains at least one malformed/undecodable %-sequence):
     return customDecodeURIComponent(encodedURI)    # -> BRANCH 2

BRANCH 2  customDecodeURIComponent(input)  (the fallback "decode as much as possible" path)
  # Build a replacement map of  run-substring -> decoded-replacement, then string-replace
  # every key globally. Insertion ORDER into the map matters (it governs replace order and
  # which overlapping key wins). The map starts PREFILLED with the two BOM pairs:
  replaceMap = ordered map:
     '%FE%FF' -> '��'    # UTF-16 BE BOM bytes -> two replacement chars
     '%FF%FE' -> '��'    # UTF-16 LE BOM bytes -> two replacement chars

  # Scan input for maximal runs of adjacent percent-escapes using multiMatcher.exec in a loop:
  FOR each run = multiMatcher run over input (left to right, maximal '(%XX)+'):
     TRY:
        replaceMap[run] = (native) decodeURIComponent(run)   # whole run decodes cleanly -> use it
     CATCH:
        result = decode(run)                                 # -> see decode() below
        IF result !== run:                                   # only record if decoding changed it;
           replaceMap[run] = result                          # if decode() couldn't improve the run,
                                                              # DO NOT add a key -> run stays LITERAL.

  # After scanning, append the special single-byte lead entry LAST so it cannot pre-empt any
  # longer valid sequence that begins with %C2 (e.g. '%C2%B5' was already inserted above and
  # wins because it is replaced first):
  replaceMap['%C2'] -> '�'    # a bare/dangling C2 lead byte -> one replacement char

  # Apply every map entry, in INSERTION ORDER, as a GLOBAL string replace:
  FOR key in Object.keys(replaceMap) (insertion order):
     input = input.replace(new RegExp(key, 'g'), replaceMap[key])
     # NOTE: keys are percent-escape strings; '%' and hex are regex-literal, so each key is a
     # plain global substring replace. Replacement VALUES are inserted literally (the source
     # passes a string, not a function; for the inputs in scope no replacement value contains a
     # '$n'/'$&' pattern that would re-expand — see SCOPE note).
  return input

decode(input)   # used by decodeComponents-driven recursion; "decode this run, salvaging max"
  TRY:
     return (native) decodeURIComponent(input)    # whole run clean -> done
  CATCH:
     tokens = input.match(singleMatcher) || []     # split run into per-escape / per-char tokens
     FOR i = 1; i < tokens.length; i++:
        input = decodeComponents(tokens, i).join('')   # try splitting the token list at index i
        tokens = input.match(singleMatcher) || []       # re-tokenize the (possibly shortened) result
     return input
  # Net effect: progressively isolates the longest decodable prefixes/suffixes; escapes that
  # cannot be decoded in any grouping are left as their literal '%XX' text.

decodeComponents(components, split=1)   # recursive binary-split decoder over a token array
  TRY:
     return [ (native) decodeURIComponent(components.join('')) ]   # this whole sub-array decodes
  CATCH: (do nothing)
  IF components.length === 1:
     return components                 # a single token that won't decode -> leave it literal
  split = split || 1
  left  = components.slice(0, split)
  right = components.slice(split)
  return [].concat(decodeComponents(left), decodeComponents(right))   # decode halves independently
  # This recursively decodes the maximal decodable groupings and leaves undecodable single
  # tokens (e.g. a lone continuation byte, a truncated lead byte) as literal '%XX'.

# =====================================================================================
# RESULTING DECISION RULE for percent-runs (the observable contract — what a blind
# engineer must reproduce; verified by running the real package):
# =====================================================================================
#  * A run (or sub-grouping) that the native decoder accepts -> its native UTF-8 decode.
#  * The exact run '%FE%FF' or '%FF%FE' anywhere -> '��' (BOM pairs, prefilled).
#  * A dangling '%C2' (the UTF-8 2-byte lead C2 not followed by a valid continuation that
#    forms a decodable char in its run) -> '�'.  (But '%C2%B5'->'µ', '%C2%A9'->'©', etc.
#    decode normally and win because the longer valid escape is mapped first.)
#  * ANY OTHER undecodable escape or fragment (lone continuation byte like '%80'/'%A4';
#    a truncated/lone lead byte like '%E2','%FE','%FF','%F0'; an overlong/invalid sequence
#    like '%C0%80'; a token whose last escape is malformed-as-text like trailing '%A' in
#    '%E0%A4%A') -> LEFT LITERALLY as its '%XX' text (NOT replaced with U+FFFD).
#  * A standalone '%' that is not a valid escape, or non-hex like '%XY','%G0' -> LEFT LITERAL.
#
# This LITERAL-vs-REPLACEMENT distinction is the most error-prone part: only the BOM pairs
# and a dangling '%C2' become U+FFFD; all other malformed bytes stay as their percent text.

# =====================================================================================
# WORKED EXAMPLES (input -> output) — produced by EXECUTING decode-uri-component@0.2.2:
# =====================================================================================
#   ''                  -> ''                       (empty; native fast path)
#   'hello world'       -> 'hello world'             (no '%'; fast path)
#   'abcDEF123'         -> 'abcDEF123'               (no '%'; fast path)
#   'café'              -> 'café'                    (already decoded; no '%'; fast path)
#   '%20'               -> ' '                       (fast path)
#   '%25'               -> '%'                       (fast path)
#   '%24'               -> '$'                       (fast path)
#   '%0A'               -> '\n'  (U+000A)            (fast path)
#   '%00'               -> '␀'                  (fast path)
#   '%7F'               -> '' (DEL)            (fast path)
#   '%C3%A9'            -> 'é'                        (2-byte UTF-8; fast path)
#   '%c3%a9'            -> 'é'                        (lowercase hex also valid; fast path)
#   '%E2%82%AC'         -> '€'                        (3-byte UTF-8; fast path)
#   '%F0%9F%98%80'      -> '😀' (U+1F600)            (4-byte UTF-8; fast path)
#   '%C2%B5'            -> 'µ'                        (fast path)
#   '%C2%A9'            -> '©'                        (fast path)
#   'a+b'               -> 'a b'                      ('+'->space; fast path)
#   'a+b+c'             -> 'a b c'                    (every '+'->space)
#   '+%XY'              -> ' %XY'                     ('+'->space, then malformed kept literal)
#   '%2B'               -> '+'                        ('%2B' is NOT literal '+', decodes to '+')
#   '%2B+'              -> '+ '                       ('%2B'->'+', literal '+'->space)
#   'a%2Bb'             -> 'a+b'
#   '%25%24'            -> '%$'                       (fast path)
#   '%25%25'            -> '%%'                       (fast path)
#   # ---- fallback (native throws on whole string) ----
#   '%'                 -> '%'                        (lone '%', no escape; unchanged)
#   '%XY'               -> '%XY'                      (non-hex; unchanged)
#   '%G0'               -> '%G0'                      (non-hex; unchanged)
#   '%XY%ZZ'            -> '%XY%ZZ'                   (unchanged)
#   '%80'               -> '%80'                      (lone continuation; LITERAL, not U+FFFD)
#   '%A4'               -> '%A4'                      (lone continuation; LITERAL)
#   '%FE'               -> '%FE'                      (lone lead; LITERAL, not U+FFFD)
#   '%FF'               -> '%FF'                      (lone lead; LITERAL)
#   '%E2'               -> '%E2'                      (truncated 3-byte; LITERAL)
#   '%E2%82'            -> '%E2%82'                   (truncated 3-byte; LITERAL)
#   '%F0'               -> '%F0'                      (truncated 4-byte; LITERAL)
#   '%F0%9F'            -> '%F0%9F'                   (LITERAL)
#   '%F0%9F%98'         -> '%F0%9F%98'                (LITERAL)
#   '%E0%A4%A'          -> '%E0%A4%A'                 (trailing '%A' not a full escape; LITERAL)
#   '%C0%80'            -> '%C0%80'                   (overlong/invalid; LITERAL)
#   '%FE%FF'            -> '��'  ('��')     (BOM pair -> two U+FFFD)
#   '%FF%FE'            -> '��'  ('��')     (BOM pair -> two U+FFFD)
#   '%FE%FF%FE%FF'      -> '����' (each BOM pair maps)
#   '%FE%FFreal'        -> '��real'  ('��real')
#   '%C2'               -> '�'  ('�')            (dangling C2 -> one U+FFFD)
#   '%C2x'              -> '�x' ('�x')           ('+'/text after; C2 dangling -> U+FFFD)
#   'a%C2b'             -> 'a�b' ('a�b')
#   'pre%C2post'        -> 'pre�post'
#   '%C2%C2'            -> '��' ('��')       (two dangling C2)
#   '%20%C2'            -> ' �' (' �')
#   '%C2%C2%B5'         -> '�µ' ('�µ')           (first C2 dangling, '%C2%B5'->'µ' wins)
#   '%C2%B5%C2'         -> 'µ�' ('µ�')
#   '%C3%A9%C2%C3%A9'   -> 'é�é' ('é�é')
#   '%FF%C3%A9'         -> '%FFé'                     ('%FF' LITERAL, '%C3%A9'->'é')
#   '%E2%82%AC%E2'      -> '€%E2'                     ('%E2%82%AC'->'€', trailing '%E2' LITERAL)
#   '%E2%28%A1'         -> '%E2(%A1'  ('%28'->'(' ; lone leads kept LITERAL)
#   'valid%20and%ZZbad' -> 'valid and%ZZbad'
#   '%st%C3%A5le%'      -> '%ståle%'                  (README example)
#   '%%7Bst%C3%A5le%7D%'-> '%{ståle}%'                (README example)
#   '%7B%ab%%7C%de%%7D' -> '{%ab%|%de%}'              (README example)
#   '%24%E2'            -> '$%E2'   ('%24'->'$', '%E2' LITERAL)
#   '%E2%24'            -> '%E2$'
#   '%26%E2'            -> '&%E2'
#   '%E0%A4%A%C3%A9'    -> '%E0%A4%Aé'

# =====================================================================================
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# Pure deterministic string->string (plus the TypeError throw-path, recorded as {__throw}).
# No clock/RNG/env/fs/net seam; nothing to inject. The fixed dependency (native
# decodeURIComponent) is a standards-fixed built-in, not an ambient seam.

SCOPE
  IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
    - the type guard + exact TypeError message (BRANCH 0)
    - global '+' -> ' ' normalization performed BEFORE decoding (BRANCH 1)
    - native fast-path inside try/catch (BRANCH 1)
    - the customDecodeURIComponent fallback: the prefilled BOM map ('%FE%FF','%FF%FE' ->
      two U+FFFD), the multiMatcher run scan, the per-run try-native / else decode() rule,
      the "only record if result !== run" guard, the appended '%C2' -> U+FFFD entry, and the
      insertion-ordered global string-replace application.
    - the decode()/decodeComponents() recursive binary-split salvage and the resulting
      LITERAL-vs-U+FFFD decision rule documented above.
    - the exact regexes: token '%[a-f0-9]{2}', singleMatcher, multiMatcher (all 'gi').
  CARRIED DATA: none. The only literals are the two BOM keys, the '%C2' key, and the two
    replacement-char values ('�' = U+FFFD) — inline constants, no attested table, no
    carried.json. The Unicode replacement character U+FFFD and UTF-8 decoding follow the
    Unicode standard / ECMAScript URI semantics; the re-emitter relies on its host's native
    decodeURIComponent for UTF-8, exactly as the source does.
  OUT-OF-CONTRACT / DOCUMENT-as-OUT (the generator does NOT emit these; they do not survive
    the oracle codec, OR their behavior is the documented throw):
    - non-string args (number, boolean, null, undefined, plain object, array, function,
      symbol, bigint): the unit THROWS TypeError with the message in BRANCH 0. This throw is
      the defined behavior (a blind re-emitter reproduces it), but the generator emits only
      string args because non-string values either don't round-trip the codec or would only
      exercise the one type-guard branch. Symbol/function/undefined are non-codec-faithful.
    - replacement VALUES containing a '$'-pattern: in principle a decoded chunk used as a
      String.replace replacement string could contain '$n'/'$&'. No in-scope input produces
      such a value in a position that re-expands (verified: '%24%E2'->'$%E2' is literal), so
      this is out of the exercised envelope; the generator does not target it.

PROVENANCE
  decode-uri-component@0.2.2
  repo: SamVerschueren/decode-uri-component (github.com/SamVerschueren/decode-uri-component)
